---
title: Supported Workflows
description: The three field workflows NAHPU supports—shared site with parallel catalogs, separate sites with parallel catalogs, and an assembly line with one catalog—and how to choose one.
sidebar:
  order: 2
---

Field teams prepare and catalog specimens in different ways. NAHPU supports
three common workflows. Choose one before fieldwork: it decides how many devices
you use, how Field IDs are assigned, what you share between devices, and whether
catalogs need merging at the end.

{% aside type="note" title="Publication context" %}
These three workflows are described, with the same letters, in Figure 2 of the
NAHPU paper: Handika, H., Moncrieff, A. E., Henderson, C., Dadaian, A.,
Gordon, C. M., … Esselstyn, J. A. *NAHPU: An integrated approach to natural
history data acquisition and management in the extended specimen era*
(manuscript). The workflow descriptions and the disciplines where each is common
follow the paper.
{% /aside %}

{% workflows label="Field workflows supported by NAHPU. Select a workflow to see how the team, the sites, and the devices fit together." %}

{% workflow id="A" short="Shared site" title="Shared site, parallel catalogs" source="One shared site and event" people="Each person catalogs on their own device" result="Merge into one project" %}

Specimens come from a shared site and collecting event—a mist net, pitfall line,
Malaise trap, or harp trap. Each person records the specimens they sort and
prepare on their own device.

- **Common in:** ornithology, terrestrial and freshwater invertebrate zoology,
  and bat fieldwork.
- **Share between devices:** the project, sites, events, people, and taxa, by QR
  code or file. QR sharing gives every device identical records.
- **Field IDs:** personnel Field IDs, one series per cataloger.
- **At the end:** merge each catalog into one project. Shared sites and events
  match instead of duplicating.
- **Learn it:** [Teamwork tutorials](/en/tutorials/teamwork/plan-and-set-up/),
  where two catalogers share Line 1 at Mt. Tujuh.

{% /workflow %}

{% workflow id="B" short="Separate sites" title="Separate sites, parallel catalogs" source="Each person runs their own line" people="Each person catalogs on their own device" result="Merge into one project" %}

Each person runs their own trap line, then prepares and catalogs the specimens
from it on their own device.

- **Common in:** non-volant small-mammal fieldwork.
- **Share between devices:** the project, people, and taxa. Each person creates
  the sites and events for their own lines; share them by QR when a teammate
  needs them before the merge.
- **Field IDs:** personnel Field IDs, one series per cataloger. Put the line
  leader's initials in the Site ID, such as `TJBS-L2`.
- **At the end:** merge each catalog into one project. Each person's own sites
  and events arrive as new records.
- **Learn it:** [Teamwork tutorial 5](/en/tutorials/teamwork/run-your-own-line/),
  where one cataloger runs his own line.

{% /workflow %}

{% workflow id="C" short="Assembly line" title="Assembly line, one catalog" source="One shared site and event" people="Each person has one role" result="One device, one catalog" %}

Specimens move through an assembly line. Each person has one role—such as
collecting tissues or photographing live animals—while one person enters every
record in a single catalog on one device.

- **Common in:** some herpetology, ichthyology, and mammalogy fieldwork.
- **Share between devices:** nothing during fieldwork. Credit each person as
  event personnel and as the `Preparator` on the parts they prepared.
- **Field IDs:** a project Field ID (prefix, number, and suffix) suits one
  device and one sequence.
- **Lots:** for fishes and invertebrates, one record can hold a lot—several
  individuals of one species—with tag IDs and individual measurements.
- **Learn it:** the [Basic tutorials](/en/tutorials/basic/paper-catalog/) use
  one device and one catalog.

{% /workflow %}

{% /workflows %}

## Compare the workflows

| | A: Shared site | B: Separate sites | C: Assembly line |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Devices | One per cataloger | One per cataloger | One |
| Sites and events | Created once, shared | Created by each line leader | Created once |
| Field IDs | Personnel | Personnel | Project or personnel |
| Merge at the end | Yes | Yes | No |
| Tutorial | [Teamwork 1–7](/en/tutorials/teamwork/plan-and-set-up/) | [Teamwork 5](/en/tutorials/teamwork/run-your-own-line/) | [Basic 1–9](/en/tutorials/basic/paper-catalog/) |

The workflows can mix. In the [Teamwork tutorials](/en/tutorials/teamwork/plan-and-set-up/),
a pair checks a shared line together (Workflow A), and one of them also runs his
own line (Workflow B); one merge handles both.

For choosing between personnel and project Field IDs, see
[Project Management](/en/best-practices/project-management/).

## Share records between devices

Workflows A and B depend on every device holding the same project, people, and
taxa, with the same UUIDs. Create them once and copy them to the other devices:

| Record | How to share it | Learn more |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Project identity | Project QR or project-info JSON | [Projects](/en/usages/projects/) |
| Sites | Site QR or JSON | [Sites](/en/usages/sites/) |
| Events | Event QR, or JSON when the QR is too large | [Collecting Events](/en/usages/events/) |
| Taxa | Taxon QR or a taxon list | [Taxon](/en/usages/taxon/) |
| People | Inside site and event QR codes and files | [Personnel](/en/usages/personnel/) |
| Specimen catalogs | Export project, then merge | [Transfer a Project](/en/usages/export/project-transfer/) |

{% aside type="caution" %}
NAHPU identifies people by UUID, not by name. Create each person once, on one
device, and let the site and event transfers carry them to the others.
Otherwise the merge sees two different people with the same name.
{% /aside %}

## Train new team members

In an assembly line, a new team member does not need to learn every role at
once. The example below follows one six-week small-mammal expedition, from the
easiest role to the hardest, with the NAHPU records each role fills.

{% skillramp label="Example training ramp over a six-week small-mammal expedition, from measuring specimens to helping manage logistics." hint="Select a role to see which NAHPU records it fills." weekLabel="Week" %}
{% skillstep title="Measure specimens" week="1" detail="Standard measurements and weight in the specimen record. With Show outlier warnings on, NAHPU flags values outside the expected range so a trainer can check them." /%}
{% skillstep title="Collect tissue samples" week="2" detail="Tissue parts with their type, treatment, and tissue ID. Parts can also record when the sample was taken and the post-mortem interval (PMI), which matter for genomic work." /%}
{% skillstep title="Trap rodents with experts" week="3" detail="Collecting events: the trap types and counts on each line, and the people who ran it. Trainees appear as event personnel, so their work is credited." /%}
{% skillstep title="Prepare wet specimens" week="4" detail="Whole animals fixed in fluid become Whole body parts with a treatment such as Formalin, with the trainee as Preparator." /%}
{% skillstep title="Prepare dry skins" week="5" detail="Study skins, skulls, and skeletons each become their own part record, so they can be stored, loaned, and tracked separately." /%}
{% skillstep title="Assist logistic management" week="6" detail="Sites, events, the field journal, daily backups, and exports. See the Best Practices guides for team conventions." /%}
{% /skillramp %}

The pace depends on the team and the taxa; use the ramp as a starting point,
not a schedule.
